00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:59.850
So, if you haven't seen the movie yet, here's
what happens.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.540
Stan "The Man" appears on a moon dressed in
an old-style space suit chatting to three

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.540
Watchers.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.040
After he complains about being so rudely interrupted,
he says "at that time I was a Federal Express

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.750
Man", a reference to his brilliant "Tony Stank"
line in Captain America: Civil War.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.850
The three characters standing around Stan
Lee are dressed like Watchers, an alien race

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.250
in Marvel's comics.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.400
As their name suggests, the Watchers' purpose
is to observe and record events throughout

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.970
the universe with a strict policy of non-intervention.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.030
Because Stan Lee has appeared in so many of
Marvel's adaptations, a popular fan theory

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.030
suggests that Stan Lee is actually a Watcher
turning up in Marvel movies and TV shows where

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.170
he watches or observes events in a cameo role.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.450
In fact, the theory suggests that Stan Lee's
character is specifically the watcher Uatu,

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:51.040
a character created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby
in 1963 for Fantastic Four #13.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.430
Uatu was the Watcher who monitored events
on Earth and its Solar System, however, he

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.140
has on occasions broken the Watchers' code
of non-interference and actually intervened

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.140
in events.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.689
Watchers are supposed to be neutral and only
chronicle the events they are observing and

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:08.440
so Uatu's interventions have sometimes landed
him in hot water.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.680
If Stan Lee's character is Uatu, it could
explain why he has sometimes got involved.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.910
For example, he tried to pull up Thor's hammer
in the first Thor movie, in Spider-Man 2 he

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.220
saved a woman from falling debris, and in
The Incredible Hulk he was inadvertently key

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:27.580
to how events unfolded as he fell ill from
gamma sickness after drinking soda contaminated

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.060
with the Hulk's blood.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.780
On many other occasions, though, Stan Lee
pops up as a bit of comedic relief generally

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.640
not affecting the plot of the movie.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.860
So, could Stan Lee be the crucial element
that connects up all of the movies and TV

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.330
shows in Marvel's live-action multiverse?

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.450
Placing Stan Lee in a scene with a group of
watchers in Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.030
seems to make this a real possibility.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:51.720
I mean Stan Lee isn't dressed like a Watcher,
but why is he there in outer space talking

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.580
to a group of other Watchers where he seems
to be telling them all about his stories and

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.910
his recent escapade on earth in Captain America:
Civil War?

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.640
Maybe because he's reporting back to the other
Watchers.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.120
By the way it's also interesting that the
Watchers have now appeared in a Marvel Studios

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.940
film, as it's previously been thought that
the rights to the Watchers or Uatu might have

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.090
been with 20th Century Fox given that the
character of Uatu first appeared in a Fantastic

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.730
Four comic and Fox own the film rights to
the Fantastic Four.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.680
If this is the case, it'll be interesting
to see if Marvel Studios did a trade or exchange

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.820
of rights for another character, like they
did with Ego The Living Planet for Guardians

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:26.820
Vol. 2.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.710
But if you thought that was it, Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 2 gives us a second scene

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.470
with Stan Lee right at the end of the credits.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.420
In the post-credits scene, the Watchers walk
off leaving Stan "The Man" by himself while

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:41.630
he pleads for them not to leave, saying "I
thought you were going to give me a ride"

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.890
and he also adds that he's still got more
stories to tell.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:48.650
That last remark is obviously a meta-joke
because Stan Lee, of course, had a hand in

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:53.390
creating many of Marvel's much-loved characters,
but it could also add fuel to the argument

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:58.750
that he wants to tell them about all his other
cameos in the Marvel movie multiverse.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:03.250
Still, it's funny how the other Watchers don't
seem very interested or impressed with Stan

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:04.250
at all!

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:08.100
Now, what do you think about Stan Lee's cameo
and post-credits scene in Guardians Vol. 2?

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.560
Do you think it proves he's really been a
Watcher all along?

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.350
Or is Marvel just joking around with a fan
theory?

